  "functions": [
    "FUNC.REGEXTEST"
  ],
  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "In REGEXTEST, \\w takes combining marks, dot refuses CR, and case folding reaches into ranges",
  "statement": "Some regular expression details in REGEXTEST differ from a plain reading. \\w matches a combining mark (U+0301) and connector punctuation (U+203F), so it is not limited to letters, digits and underscore. Dot does not match CR as well as LF: REGEXTEST(\"a\"&CHAR(13)&\"b\",\"a.b\") is FALSE. \\d does not match a Han character. With case_sensitivity 1, folding applies inside a character class range: É matches [é-é] and Σ matches [α-ω]. It also covers the non-ASCII partners of ASCII letters: the Kelvin sign U+212A matches k and long s U+017F matches s. ß matches its capital U+1E9E, and a Deseret letter above U+FFFF matches its other case.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "12 recorded REGEXTEST readings, in a workbook at compatibility version 2.",
  "inferred": "The readings cover the characters named here. That \\w follows the Unicode Mn and Pc categories in general, and that ranges fold by simple case folding in general, is inferred.",
